[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I recently started 3D printing parts for my Land Cruiser, and one of the most popular questions was: will they melt? So, today we\'ll figure it out together. It must be about 39 degrees outside, and we both know it\'s'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'much hotter inside the car. Look at this. This is my dashboard, and it\'s about 93 degrees here. So for the test, I printed the same parts for the Land Cruiser from different'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'materials and subjected them to the same heat test. And we\'ll see which one is the best. Let\'s start with the simplest material. PLA is one of the most common and cheapest materials for 3D'

[00:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'printing, and it is great for prototypes, decor, and indoor parts where you don\'t need to worry about high temperatures. But it\'s not really known for its suitability for functional tasks. So I didn\'t expect much from this option. I'

[00:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'left them in a hot car for a few hours, and things started to change quickly. As you can see, he has become quite pliable. Next comes PETG. It is considered more heat-resistant than PLA, which is'

[01:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'why it is often recommended when you need to print something more durable. However, when the temperature rose to 90 degrees, the PETG began to soften and'

[01:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'eventually sag. Look how hot it got in the car. So much so that my phone refused to shoot the timelapse I wanted. This is where the fun begins. I\'ve already'

[01:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'printed a few parts for another Land Cruiser of mine with PETG, and they stayed in the car all summer when the temperature reached 40-41 degrees, and they didn\'t melt. So why was this particular part'

[01:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'deformed? The fact is that the temperature exceeded 85 degrees, and it is at this temperature that PETG begins to become soft. So if you\'re printing with PETG, this is something to keep in mind. Next up are ASA and ABS. They'

[01:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'heated up to 74 and 80 degrees, respectively. And everything was fine with them . The reason is that these materials can withstand temperatures up to 100 degrees before becoming soft and pliable. This is'

[02:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'probably why most people recommend ABS and ASA for car parts. They are designed to operate at higher temperatures than PLA. And ASA also has the advantage of being better suited for outdoor use . So if you\'re'

[02:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'printing something that will be in a car, like my phone holder, ASA is the best choice. And finally, we have PAHTCF. This is high-temperature nylon reinforced with carbon fiber. Also known by'

[02:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'its scientific name PAHT. That\'s right. This material is in a completely different category than the others I tested, because it is designed for high temperatures and heavy loads. It is'

[02:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'specifically positioned for automotive components and engine assemblies. So I expected him to do well. And it did a great job, because the deformation temperature is'

[02:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'between 293 and 381 degrees, which is simply incredible.'
